  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n18z/1pm surface analysis shows a 1025mb high centered over the\nDakotas...while a stationary frontal boundary remains draped\nfrom central Ohio southwestward to the Texas panhandle. Several\nweak waves have been tracking along the front today, resulting\nin scattered light showers across the southern half of the KEAX\nCWA. \n\nAs a vigorous upper low evident on the latest water vapor\nimagery over western Colorado pivots northeastward, surface\ncyclogenesis will occur across northern Texas tonight...with the\nlow tracking into northern Indiana by Thursday evening. Rich \ndeep-layer moisture is currently in place as evidenced by NAM \nprecipitable water values of 1.75 to 2 inches. As stronger \nforcing approaches from the west, showers will become more \nnumerous later tonight into Thursday morning. CAMs have trended \nslower with the precip with general consensus suggesting only \nscattered showers until after 09z/4am. Based on the \njuxtaposition of strongest lift and deepest moisture, the most \nwidespread rainfall will focus between 09z/4am and 15z/10am. \nWhile the strongest instability will remain further south in \ncloser proximity to the front, NAM MUCAPE values increase to \n200-400J/kg...supporting a few rumbles of thunder. Once the \ninitial forcing lifts N/NE of the area, the rain will diminish \nto scattered showers by late Thursday morning. Additional \nrainfall through Thursday will range from around 0.50 near the \nIowa border to 1 to 1.25 across the far southern counties in the\nFlood Watch.\n\nOnce the surface low tracks into the Great Lakes, it will pull a\nstrong cold front through the area Thursday afternoon...resulting\nin an end to the showers and introducing a much cooler airmass\nfor the end of the week. As Canadian high pressure builds\nsouthward, strong/gusty northwesterly winds will develop behind\nthe departing front Thursday night into Friday. Friday morning\nwill be the windiest period with the 13z NBM indicating a 40-70%\nchance of gusts greater than 30mph. After high temperatures \nonly in the 50s, clear skies and diminishing winds will allow \nlows to dip into the lower to middle 30s Friday night. While \nthere is still some uncertainty concerning wind speeds, \nconfidence continues to grow for widespread frost by early \nSaturday morning. After that, the cool fall weather will \ncontinue through the weekend...followed by a return to above \nnormal temps in the lower to middle 70s by next Tuesday/Wednesday.",
